editorialMd:
Build the result list

Put all numbers from readings into a min-heap. A min-heap is a tree-shaped collection that keeps its smallest number at the top. Remove the smallest number, record it, and repeat until the heap is empty. Keep repeated numbers. The result list is the removal order.

Return the answer

Count the even numbers in the result list. An even number divides by 2 with no remainder. Count 0 and negative even numbers too. Return the count. An empty list gives 0.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n log n); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function priorityBasketEvenKeyCensus(readings, k) {
  const a=readings; let out=[];
  const heap=[];for(const x of a){heap.push(x);let i=heap.length-1;while(i){const p=(i-1)>>1;if(heap[p]<=heap[i])break;[heap[p],heap[i]]=[heap[i],heap[p]];i=p;}}while(heap.length){out.push(heap[0]);const last=heap.pop();if(!heap.length)break;heap[0]=last;let i=0;while(true){let j=i,l=2*i+1,r=l+1;if(l<heap.length&&heap[l]<heap[j])j=l;if(r<heap.length&&heap[r]<heap[j])j=r;if(j===i)break;[heap[i],heap[j]]=[heap[j],heap[i]];i=j;}}
  return out.filter(x=>x%2===0).length;
}


